      "explanation": "<p> <img src=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1l3hf6b3o/7a43a948-6d10-4eb8-9882-d0d5582ecf46/ef80b740-d9de-11ec-8ea5-5be6b5e3368b/file-1l3hf6b3p.png?format=png\" data-orsrc=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/image/1l3hf6b3o/7a43a948-6d10-4eb8-9882-d0d5582ecf46/ef80b740-d9de-11ec-8ea5-5be6b5e3368b/file-1l3hf6b3p.png\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"max-width:100%;height:auto;display:block;margin:0px auto;max-height:40vh\" alt=\"JEE Main 2021 (Online) 17th March Evening Shift Chemistry - Solutions Question 122 English Explanation\" srcset=\"https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/500/image/1l3hf6b3o/7a43a948-6d10-4eb8-9882-d0d5582ecf46/ef80b740-d9de-11ec-8ea5-5be6b5e3368b/file-1l3hf6b3p.png?format=png 500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/750/image/1l3hf6b3o/7a43a948-6d10-4eb8-9882-d0d5582ecf46/ef80b740-d9de-11ec-8ea5-5be6b5e3368b/file-1l3hf6b3p.png?format=png 750w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1000/image/1l3hf6b3o/7a43a948-6d10-4eb8-9882-d0d5582ecf46/ef80b740-d9de-11ec-8ea5-5be6b5e3368b/file-1l3hf6b3p.png?format=png 1000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/1500/image/1l3hf6b3o/7a43a948-6d10-4eb8-9882-d0d5582ecf46/ef80b740-d9de-11ec-8ea5-5be6b5e3368b/file-1l3hf6b3p.png?format=png 1500w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2000/image/1l3hf6b3o/7a43a948-6d10-4eb8-9882-d0d5582ecf46/ef80b740-d9de-11ec-8ea5-5be6b5e3368b/file-1l3hf6b3p.png?format=png 2000w, https://app-content.cdn.examgoal.net/fly/2500/image/1l3hf6b3o/7a43a948-6d10-4eb8-9882-d0d5582ecf46/ef80b740-d9de-11ec-8ea5-5be6b5e3368b/file-1l3hf6b3p.png?format=png 2500w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 720px\" decoding=\"async\"> </p>\n<p>Effective molality = 0.6 + 1.6 + 0.4 = 2.6 m</p>\n<p>As elevation in boiling point is a colligative property which depends on the amount of solute. So, to have same boiling point, the molality of two solutions should be same.</p>\n<p>Molality of non-electrolyte solution = molality of <mjx-container class=\"